<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Consuetudines et Iusticie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1091</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Consuetudines et Iusticie is a record of the rights of the duke of Normandy, drawn up from an inquest held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> in July 1091 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a>, based on the customs believed to have held under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>. It restricted private war, castle building and the minting of coin without ducal consent, and is a key source for Norman ducal power.</p></div>
